\name{mlr_pipeops_fcst.fourier}
\alias{mlr_pipeops_fcst.fourier}
\alias{PipeOpFcstFourier}
\title{Create Fourier Features for Seasonality}
\description{
Creates pairs of Fourier (harmonic) terms \code{sin(2 * pi * k * t / period)} and \code{cos(2 * pi * k * t / period)} as new
feature columns, for \verb{k = 1, ..., K} harmonics per seasonal \code{period}, where \code{t} is the per-series time position. They
encode seasonality as a flexible alternative to seasonal lags, in particular for long or non-integer periods and
multiple seasonalities at once.
}
\section{Parameters}{

The parameters are the parameters inherited from \link[mlr3pipelines:PipeOpTaskPreprocSimple]{mlr3pipelines::PipeOpTaskPreprocSimple}, as well as the following
parameters:
\itemize{
\item \code{period} :: \code{character()} | \code{numeric()} | \code{NULL}\cr
Seasonal period(s), in number of observations per cycle, or cycle name(s) such as \code{"year"} resolved against the
task's frequency. May be non-integer and may contain multiple periods for multiple seasonalities. If \code{NULL}
(default), the default derived from the task's \code{freq} is used.
\item \code{K} :: \code{integer()}\cr
Number of Fourier harmonics per \code{period}. Either a single value recycled to all periods, or one value per period.
Each \code{K} must satisfy \code{2 * K <= period}. Default \code{1L}.
}
}

\section{Seasonal period}{

The seasonal period is not stored in the task.
It is set by the \code{period} hyperparameter, in observations per cycle.
Either a positive number such as \code{12}, or a cycle name resolved against the task's \code{freq} such as \code{"year"} or
\code{"week"}.
The cycle names a calendar \code{freq} implies and the periods they resolve to are:\tabular{lllll}{
   \code{freq} \tab \code{"hour"} \tab \code{"day"} \tab \code{"week"} \tab \code{"year"} \cr
   \code{"min"} \tab 60 \tab 1440 \tab 10080 \tab 525960 \cr
   \code{"hour"} \tab  \tab 24 \tab 168 \tab 8766 \cr
   \code{"day"} \tab  \tab  \tab 7 \tab 365.25 \cr
   \code{"week"} \tab  \tab  \tab  \tab 52.18 \cr
   \code{"month"} \tab  \tab  \tab  \tab 12 \cr
   \code{"quarter"} \tab  \tab  \tab  \tab 4 \cr
}


A multiple of a unit divides the period, e.g. \code{"30 min"} resolves \code{"day"} to \code{48}.
If \code{period} is \code{NULL} (default), it is the day for sub-daily data (the hour below a minute), the week for daily
data, and the year for longer steps, skipping a cycle of fewer than four observations for the next longer one.
This gives e.g. \code{12} for monthly, \code{4} for quarterly, \code{7} for daily, \code{24} for hourly, and \code{48} for half-hourly data.
Without a calendar \code{freq}, the default is \code{1}, i.e. no seasonality.
}

